# (V) Misc
# Show a little difference between the query after elimination of LEFT
# JOIN t3, and the query if it had no LEFT JOIN t3 to begin with: in
# the first case, the nest (t2-t3) becomes (t2), whereas in the second
# case, (t2) becomes just t2 as an effect of simplify_joins().
# simplify_joins() runs before join elimination which is why, in the
# first case, (t2) remains (t2). We could run simplify_joins() a
# second time after join elimination, if something has been
# eliminated, but such function is not quite re-rentrant which leads
# to crashes. And it is not worth it: the Optimizer works just as well
# with (t2) as with just t2, as we see in the identical query plans.
EXPLAIN SELECT DISTINCT t1.a,t2.a FROM t1 LEFT JOIN (t2 LEFT JOIN t2 AS t3 ON TRUE) ON TRUE;
